1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:02,050
Three different games.

2
00:00:02,070 --> 00:00:03,250
The same crash,

3
00:00:03,270 --> 00:00:04,130
on the same line

4
00:00:04,150 --> 00:00:05,650
of a sound library.

5
00:00:05,670 --> 00:00:07,410
No single store console would

6
00:00:07,430 --> 00:00:08,480
ever show you that.

7
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:11,500
We are Rui Codex,

8
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:13,180
a small studio in Belgium,

9
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:15,020
with 34 apps on Google

10
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:16,620
Play and the App Store.

11
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:18,700
Our feedback is not a flood.

12
00:00:18,720 --> 00:00:20,060
It is a trickle:

13
00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:22,780
51 written reviews in 9 languages,

14
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:25,820
42 crash issues and 68 releases,

15
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:27,660
scattered across two stores and

16
00:00:27,680 --> 00:00:29,580
a crash console nobody opens

17
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:30,410
on a Monday.

18
00:00:31,560 --> 00:00:32,980
So during this hackathon

19
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:34,980
we built Storefront Steward:

20
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:36,020
an agent that runs

21
00:00:36,040 --> 00:00:37,780
the studio's morning pass,

22
00:00:37,800 --> 00:00:39,140
with one rule.

23
00:00:39,160 --> 00:00:40,580
Nothing reaches a store

24
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:41,970
without a human click.

25
00:00:42,940 --> 00:00:44,760
One click starts the pass.

26
00:00:44,780 --> 00:00:46,120
Muse Spark plans it,

27
00:00:46,140 --> 00:00:47,640
and calls our own tools:

28
00:00:47,660 --> 00:00:49,320
a read-only store sync,

29
00:00:49,340 --> 00:00:52,920
triage, grouping, reply drafts and tickets.

30
00:00:52,940 --> 00:00:54,520
Every step, and its cost,

31
00:00:54,540 --> 00:00:55,990
appears as it happens.

32
00:00:56,930 --> 00:00:57,870
Every review,

33
00:00:57,890 --> 00:00:58,990
crash and release of the

34
00:00:59,010 --> 00:01:00,270
whole studio fits in one

35
00:01:00,290 --> 00:01:02,270
Muse Spark context.

36
00:01:02,290 --> 00:01:03,550
No retrieval.

37
00:01:03,570 --> 00:01:06,350
It returns issues as strict JSON,

38
00:01:06,370 --> 00:01:07,870
and plain code checks

39
00:01:07,890 --> 00:01:09,310
every cited id,

40
00:01:09,330 --> 00:01:10,430
and every quote,

41
00:01:10,450 --> 00:01:11,390
word for word,

42
00:01:11,410 --> 00:01:13,150
before anything is shown.

43
00:01:13,170 --> 00:01:15,390
It also remembers the last pass,

44
00:01:15,410 --> 00:01:16,030
so it can tell

45
00:01:16,050 --> 00:01:17,100
you what is new.

46
00:01:17,880 --> 00:01:19,380
Then it drafts replies,

47
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:21,540
in the reviewer's own language.

48
00:01:21,560 --> 00:01:22,180
Here,

49
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:24,020
Spark promised that ads would

50
00:01:24,040 --> 00:01:26,420
become skippable within five seconds.

51
00:01:26,440 --> 00:01:27,700
Only a release note

52
00:01:27,720 --> 00:01:29,220
may promise a feature.

53
00:01:29,240 --> 00:01:31,300
The verifier blocked the draft,

54
00:01:31,320 --> 00:01:32,900
sent the reason back once,

55
00:01:32,920 --> 00:01:34,450
and the revision passed.

56
00:01:35,310 --> 00:01:37,450
On 120 held-out reviews

57
00:01:37,470 --> 00:01:38,810
in 10 languages,

58
00:01:38,830 --> 00:01:42,090
117 of 119 drafts

59
00:01:42,110 --> 00:01:43,690
passed first time.

60
00:01:43,710 --> 00:01:45,210
Building that test found a

61
00:01:45,230 --> 00:01:46,810
defect in our own prompt,

62
00:01:46,830 --> 00:01:48,090
and a false alarm

63
00:01:48,110 --> 00:01:49,320
in our own checker.

64
00:01:50,140 --> 00:01:52,600
For engineering, it writes the ticket.

65
00:01:52,620 --> 00:01:54,040
One fix in the sound

66
00:01:54,060 --> 00:01:56,150
library reaches three games.

67
00:01:56,220 --> 00:01:56,920
Approve it,

68
00:01:56,940 --> 00:01:57,880
and it is exported

69
00:01:57,900 --> 00:01:58,870
for the tracker.

70
00:01:59,580 --> 00:02:00,600
For the store listing,

71
00:02:00,620 --> 00:02:03,160
Muse Image paints only the scene.

72
00:02:03,180 --> 00:02:05,320
SAM finds the phone's screen,

73
00:02:05,340 --> 00:02:06,600
and our code places

74
00:02:06,620 --> 00:02:08,520
the untouched app capture.

75
00:02:08,540 --> 00:02:11,480
A guard compares every interface pixel.

76
00:02:11,500 --> 00:02:13,320
The difference must be zero,

77
00:02:13,340 --> 00:02:15,190
or the image is thrown away.

78
00:02:16,610 --> 00:02:18,190
Personal data is removed

79
00:02:18,210 --> 00:02:19,790
before any model call.

80
00:02:19,810 --> 00:02:21,550
And triage itself runs

81
00:02:21,570 --> 00:02:22,830
on open weights:

82
00:02:22,850 --> 00:02:25,870
we distilled Spark into Muse Glimmer.

83
00:02:25,890 --> 00:02:30,670
0.993 macro-F1, against the teacher's 0.991,

84
00:02:30,690 --> 00:02:32,430
at a fifth of the cost.

85
00:02:32,450 --> 00:02:33,630
It even runs on

86
00:02:33,650 --> 00:02:34,910
a single PC,

87
00:02:34,930 --> 00:02:36,140
with no network.

88
00:02:37,020 --> 00:02:38,280
Every call is priced

89
00:02:38,300 --> 00:02:39,400
before it is sent,

90
00:02:39,420 --> 00:02:41,400
and written to a receipt.

91
00:02:41,420 --> 00:02:42,840
The whole build used under

92
00:02:42,860 --> 00:02:45,590
$10 of Meta API credit.

93
00:02:47,230 --> 00:02:48,570
Storefront Steward.

94
00:02:48,590 --> 00:02:50,010
The studio's feedback,

95
00:02:50,030 --> 00:02:51,130
read in one place,

96
00:02:51,150 --> 00:02:52,920
and answered with a human click.
